package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part mystery about a travelling book cart and a missing town archive. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch41 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_cart_that_knew_the_way",
            title = "The Cart That Knew the Way",
            tagline = "A quiet library volunteer follows a trail of book labels to uncover a forgotten promise.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "The Tuesday Route", listOf(
                    "Every Tuesday afternoon, the library's little blue book cart travelled from the front desk to the children's room, then to the reading garden, and finally back to the returns shelf. It was pushed by volunteers, not by a motor, and its wheels squeaked at every turn. When Amara began helping at the library, she learned the route quickly. What she did not understand was why the cart sometimes carried a book with a yellow label that did not appear in the library catalogue. The label showed a tiny drawing of a compass and the words 'Return where the story began.'",
                    "The first time Amara noticed it, she assumed the book had been placed on the wrong shelf. She took it to the librarian, Mrs Eze, who checked the label and the book's inside cover. There was no library stamp, no borrower's name, and no barcode. Mrs Eze asked Amara not to remove it from the building until they understood its origin. The book was a collection of local folktales, carefully repaired and wrapped in plain blue paper. On the final page, someone had pencilled a date from twenty years earlier and the initials 'M.A.'",
                    "Amara mentioned the unusual label to her friend Femi, who volunteered in the library's archive room. He recognised the compass drawing from a box of old community reading-project materials. The project had sent books to neighbourhood gathering places before the library opened its new branch. Some of those books had never been formally returned. Femi suggested they ask Mrs Eze whether the archive box could be examined. Amara looked at the cart waiting beside the desk. Perhaps its Tuesday route was not as ordinary as everyone thought."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Archive Box", listOf(
                    "Mrs Eze unlocked the archive room and brought out a labelled box marked COMMUNITY READING ROUTE. Inside were paper maps, volunteer schedules, photographs, and lists of books that had travelled between neighbourhood centres. The project had been organised by a retired teacher named Mr Akin, who believed that a library should visit people as well as wait for people to visit it. Volunteers had pushed carts to a clinic waiting room, a youth centre, and a small riverside hall. The records showed that the project ended when the old cart broke and the volunteers became too few to continue.",
                    "Femi found a page with the same compass symbol. It described a simple system: each travelling book received a yellow label showing the place where it was first read aloud. The label was not a secret code. It helped volunteers return books to the correct community collection after each visit. The folktale book in the cart had been listed under 'Riverside Hall — shelf two'. Amara wondered why it had returned to the library after so many years. The box contained no note explaining that part.",
                    "A photograph showed Mr Akin standing beside the original blue cart. A young volunteer held the folktale collection, and a second person held a notebook with the initials M.A. written on its cover. Mrs Eze said the young volunteer might have been Mariam Adeyemi, who later became a teacher. She had helped the library organise its first mobile reading days. The students decided to ask the archive committee for permission to contact her through the public school office. They wanted to return the book properly, not turn a routine library record into gossip."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "The Riverside Hall", listOf(
                    "The library contacted the current manager of Riverside Hall and arranged a visit with Mrs Eze. The building had been renovated, but a small reading corner remained near the windows. The manager remembered that older residents sometimes spoke about a travelling book cart. He checked a cupboard containing donated books and found a shelf label with the same compass symbol. The shelf itself was empty, but a framed photograph showed children gathered around a storyteller. The folktale collection in the library's cart appeared in the photograph, resting on a low table.",
                    "The manager explained that the reading corner had been moved during renovations. Some books were donated to the new library branch, while others were placed in storage. He had no record of the blue-wrapped collection. Amara showed him the yellow label and asked whether the hall had a volunteer notebook. He found a folder of public project reports, including a list of reading sessions. One entry mentioned that the collection should remain available to children who visited after school. Another note said that a volunteer would bring it back after the next festival.",
                    "The next festival had been cancelled because of heavy rain, and the project records stopped there. Femi noticed that the notebook had a page missing between the festival plan and the final inventory. It might have contained the return record. The manager offered to ask the hall's trustees whether any older documents had been moved to their storage office. Amara thanked him and wrote down the facts they had confirmed: the book had been used at the hall, the project had ended, and its final location was unknown. They still did not know why it had appeared on the library cart."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "A Name in the Margin", listOf(
                    "A week later, the school office replied that Mariam Adeyemi was now the head teacher at a nearby primary school. Mrs Eze sent a respectful message explaining the discovery and asking whether Mariam would be willing to discuss the old reading project. Mariam agreed to meet them at the library after school. She arrived carrying a notebook with a familiar compass symbol on its cover. When she saw the yellow label, she smiled with recognition but also looked thoughtful.",
                    "Mariam explained that the folktale collection had belonged to a small group of volunteer readers. They used it during visits to the riverside hall because several children asked for the same stories each week. The collection was never intended to be owned by one person. It was meant to stay with the reading route. When the project ended, Mariam took the book home temporarily to repair its loose pages. She planned to return it after the festival, but then her family moved and the book travelled with their belongings. She had kept it safe, but over time she forgot the exact shelf where it belonged.",
                    "Amara asked how the book had reached the library cart. Mariam said she had donated it during a recent clear-out, adding the old label because she thought the library might recognise the symbol. She had not realised the cart was still following a route connected to the original project. Mrs Eze checked the donation form and confirmed that the book had been placed on the cart by a volunteer who noticed its label. The mystery was nearly solved, but Mariam had one more request: she wanted the book to return to the riverside hall for a public reading, if the trustees agreed."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Missing Page", listOf(
                    "The trustees approved a public reading and invited the library to help. Before the event, Femi reviewed the archive box again. He found a thin envelope tucked beneath the bottom cardboard layer. Inside was the missing page from the volunteer notebook. It recorded the final festival plan and explained that Mariam had been asked to keep the folktale collection safe until the reading route could restart. The project had not ended because the volunteers stopped caring. They had paused it while the community centre was being rebuilt, expecting to begin again when the space reopened.",
                    "The note included a list of people who had agreed to help with the restart. Some had moved away, while others were still active in local schools and community groups. Mrs Eze checked the names against the library's public volunteer records and contacted the project coordinator through the appropriate organisations. Several people replied that they would be happy to help with a new reading day. The discovery changed the plan from a one-time return into a possible revival of the whole route.",
                    "Amara noticed that the old schedule included a stop at a clinic waiting room. She asked whether the new project could offer books in large print and a small selection in local languages. Mariam liked the idea and suggested asking readers what they would actually use rather than guessing. The group added a planning meeting to the calendar. The cart had brought them to a missing page, but the page had revealed something larger: a promise that had been delayed, not abandoned."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "A Route for Everyone", listOf(
                    "At the planning meeting, volunteers spread the old map across a table and compared it with the current neighbourhood map. Some streets had changed names, and the clinic had moved to a new building. The group agreed not to follow the old route blindly. They would ask each location for permission, check access arrangements, and choose times that did not interfere with services. The library's accessibility adviser recommended a lighter cart, clear shelf labels, and a printed list so visitors could browse without needing a phone or internet connection.",
                    "Mariam suggested that each stop should have a small collection selected with help from people who used that place. The clinic could request calm short reads, the youth centre could choose science and adventure books, and the riverside hall could select folktales and local history. Amara volunteered to organise a feedback card that did not ask for names or private information. Femi created a simple inventory sheet so the team could track where each book belonged and when it should return.",
                    "The group tested the cart inside the library first. Its wheels still squeaked, but it moved safely and could turn through the wider aisles. A volunteer noticed that the yellow labels were difficult to read from a distance, so Amara redesigned them with larger lettering and a clear location name. They kept the compass symbol as a nod to the original project. By the end of the afternoon, the cart was ready for its first approved visit. It would not be a mysterious journey. It would be a carefully planned service built around the people who wanted it."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The First New Visit", listOf(
                    "The first new visit took place at the youth centre. Staff had chosen books about astronomy, inventions, and short mysteries. The volunteers arrived at the agreed time, checked in with the coordinator, and set the cart beside a table without blocking the entrance. Amara explained how the borrowing cards worked and showed visitors the large-print labels. A younger student picked a book about the night sky and asked whether the library had anything about planets beyond the solar system. Femi wrote the suggestion on the anonymous feedback sheet.",
                    "The visit went smoothly until a wheel caught on a loose edge in the floor covering. The cart stopped, and one stack of books leaned sideways. No one was hurt, but the volunteers decided to pause and move the cart only after the centre staff had secured the edge. The accessibility adviser later recommended a different route through the room. Amara was glad they had tested the cart before carrying it to every location. A service was only useful if it could be used safely and comfortably.",
                    "At the end of the visit, the coordinator asked whether the cart could return every month. The library could not promise that immediately, but Mrs Eze said they would review volunteer availability and transport needs. The team recorded the request without making a promise they might not keep. On the way back, Amara noticed that the cart's route had changed from the old map. It now followed the accessible path through the building. The project was honouring its history by adapting, not by pretending the neighbourhood had stayed the same."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "The Reading at Riverside", listOf(
                    "The riverside reading day brought together former volunteers, current library members, and families from the neighbourhood. Mariam placed the repaired folktale collection on the cart and read the first story aloud. She explained that the book had travelled through many hands and that its yellow label recorded a place, not an owner. A child asked whether the compass meant the book was lost. Mariam answered that it meant the volunteers had a way to help it find its next reader.",
                    "After the reading, visitors explored the new collection and suggested titles for future visits. One parent asked for books that could be read together by adults and younger children. A retired resident offered to record a short introduction to a local folktale, provided the library reviewed it and obtained permission before sharing it. Mrs Eze added the idea to the planning list. The team did not record anyone spontaneously; they agreed to arrange a proper session with clear consent and a chance to review the finished audio.",
                    "The trustees thanked the library and asked whether the old reading corner could become a regular stop again. The library agreed to discuss a schedule after checking staff and volunteer capacity. Mariam returned the folktale collection to the cart, where its label now read 'Riverside Hall — shared reading shelf'. Amara felt a quiet satisfaction. The book had come back to the place where it belonged, but it had also helped people make a new plan together."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "The Cart's New Map", listOf(
                    "Over the next month, the volunteers reviewed feedback from the youth centre and riverside hall. They added astronomy books, large-print stories, and a small collection of local history. The clinic requested short books with clear type and no complicated borrowing process. The library made a separate route plan for each location and included contact details for the staff member who would receive the cart. Amara designed a new map with clear symbols and a note that visits could change when a location needed privacy or quiet.",
                    "Femi compared the old inventory with the new one. Several books from the original project had been donated to schools, while others were still in the library archive. The team decided not to reclaim them. The purpose was to make reading easier, not to gather every object connected to the past. They added a history page to the library display explaining what was known and what remained uncertain. Mariam reviewed it and corrected one date that had been copied incorrectly in an old report.",
                    "The library board approved a three-month pilot with visits to the youth centre, clinic, and riverside hall. They set a review date and agreed to measure practical things: whether the schedule was manageable, whether the collections matched requests, and whether the cart could be moved safely. Amara pinned the map beside the returns shelf. The blue cart now had a route that everyone could understand, and no one needed to invent a mystery to make it meaningful."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "Where the Story Began", listOf(
                    "At the end of the pilot, the library reviewed the feedback with representatives from each location. The youth centre wanted a monthly visit, the clinic preferred a smaller collection every six weeks, and the riverside hall offered to host a reading afternoon each term. The board approved the schedule because volunteers had confirmed they could support it. The cart would not visit every place every Tuesday, but its new route was reliable and based on real needs.",
                    "Mariam donated the original project notebook to the archive, where it was stored with the meeting records and the recovered missing page. The folktale collection stayed in the shared reading rotation, with a clear label and a record of its location. Amara wrote a short display note explaining the book's journey without turning Mariam's temporary mistake into blame. She thanked the former volunteers for keeping the collection safe and the current team for giving it a new purpose.",
                    "On the next Tuesday afternoon, the blue cart left the returns shelf and followed its updated map. Its wheels still squeaked, but the sound no longer suggested a puzzle waiting to be solved. It announced a service beginning another round. Amara watched a young reader choose the folktale collection and sit beside the window. The book had returned where its story began, but it had not gone backwards. It had found a way to keep travelling, carrying the promise of a library that could meet people wherever they were."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
